## Actor programs

Use TypeScript snippets for one-time work. Actors can own functions, shared state and React views.
{{#if (available "actor_program_create")}}
Create the closest template with `actor_program_create`, read its files in the returned `directory`,
adapt what is needed{{#if (available "actor_program_activate")}}, then `actor_program_activate`{{/if}}. Reuse its code and tests{{#if (available "bash")}}; `bash` runs there with `cwd: "@actors/<name>"`,
in a room `cwd: "@actors/<room>.<name>"`{{/if}}.
{{/if}}
Call these functions directly; `typescript_api` gives the current contracts. Published actor
functions join your tools during the current turn.
Bind existing actors with `self` or `@handle`. A backend without a binding creates a TypeScript
actor; a pure view binds to the calling actor. UI functions never require a model turn.
Changed project errors arrive automatically{{#if (available "actor_program_diagnostics")}}; `actor_program_diagnostics` gives full diagnostics{{/if}}.
A visible view is already open as a tab beside Chat; it needs no address or browser.
{{#if (available "actor_view_set_visibility")}}
`actor_view_set_visibility` hides or shows it by its `ref`; hiding keeps state.
{{/if}}
{{#if (available "actor_program_controls")}}
Query `actor_program_controls` when specific props are unclear, `topic: "guide"` for the workflow,
`topic: "state-aware"` for a `defineProgram` that waits for a time, a message or a file.
{{else}}
Work that waits for a time, a message or a file and then continues is a `defineProgram`.
{{/if}}
Controls are shadcn/ui components with their usual props, icons from `lucide-react`, Tailwind for layout.
Build views with `AppLayout`, `Stack` and responsive `Grid`; the host supplies base typography.
Use `Form` for labeled inputs, including `type: "textarea"`; do not rebuild field wrappers with custom CSS.
For diagrams, adapt the `controls` SVG example (`SvgEdge`); nodes stay Tailwind app code.
